      "text": "To solve the problem, we first need to understand the geometry of the hexagon and the transformation involved. Let's start by placing the hexagon in the complex plane.\n\nA regular hexagon centered at the origin with opposite sides one unit apart and one pair of sides parallel to the imaginary axis can be thought of as having vertices at the following points:\n\\[ \\pm \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right), \\pm \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right), \\pm \\left( -\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right) \\]\nby rotating the standard hexagon \\(60^\\circ\\) clockwise around the origin. The vertices are:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{1}{2} - i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2} - i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{1}{2} - i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2} - i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\]\n\nFor \\(z = x + iy\\), the transformation \\(w = \\frac{1}{z}\\) maps the point \\(z\\) to \\(\\frac{1}{x + iy} = \\frac{x - iy}{x^2 + y^2}\\). We need to find the image of the region \\(R\\) outside the hexagon. The region \\(S\\) is obtained by mapping the region \\(R\\).\n\nThe image of the region \\(R\\) under the transformation \\(w = \\frac{1}{z}\\) is the region within a circle of radius \\(\\frac{1}{\\text{radius of the hexagon}} = 2\\), but excluding the origin. This is because the transformation maps the upper half of the plane to the exterior of a circle of radius 2 and the lower half of the plane to the interior of the circle centered at the origin.\n\nThe area of the circle with radius 2 is:\n\\[ \\pi \\times 2^2 = 4\\pi \\]\nHowever, the area of the region we are interested in is the area of the annulus (the region between the circle and the origin) which is:\n\\[ 4\\pi - \\pi \\times 0^2 = 4\\pi - 0 = 4\\pi - \\pi = 4\\pi \\]\n\nThus, the area of \\(S\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{4\\pi} \\]",
