      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by understanding the geometry and properties of the regular hexagon and its transformation by inversion through the point at infinity.\n\n1. **Identify the side length of the hexagon:**\n\n   The hexagon is regular and its opposite pairs of sides are one unit apart. Since the hexagon is symmetric and centered at the origin, the center is the origin \\(0\\). The distance between two diagonally opposite vertices of the hexagon is the distance between \\(1\\) and \\(-1\\), which is \\(2\\). The side length \\(s\\) of the hexagon is half of this distance, so:\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Find the radius of the hexagon:**\n\n   The radius \\(r\\) of the circumscribed circle of the hexagon (which passes through the vertices of the hexagon) is equal to the side length \\(s\\). Therefore, the radius \\(r\\) is:\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the region \\(R\\):**\n\n   The region \\(R\\) is the exterior of the hexagon. In the complex plane, the circle with radius \\(r = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) centered at the origin is the boundary of \\(R\\).\n\n4. **Transform the region \\(R\\) through inversion:**\n\n   Consider the inversion \\(S = \\left\\lbrace\\frac{1}{z} \\ | \\ z \\in R\\right\\rbrace\\). This transformation maps a point \\(z\\) in the exterior of the circle of radius \\(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) to the point \\(1/z\\).\n\n5. **Analyze the transformed region \\(S\\):**\n\n   The inversion of the circle \\(|z| = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) is a circle of radius \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) centered at the origin. Therefore, the region \\(S = \\left\\lbrace\\frac{1}{z} \\ | \\ z \\in R\\right\\rbrace\\) is the exterior of the circle of radius \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) centered at the origin.\n\n6. **Calculate the area of \\(S\\):**\n\n   The area of a circle is given by \\(\\pi r^2\\). For the circle of radius \\(\\sqrt{3}\\), the area is:\n   \\[\n   \\pi (\\sqrt{3})^2 = 3\\pi\n   \\]\n\nThus, the area of the region \\(S\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3\\pi}\n\\]",
